Analyze the uploaded storyboard image. It contains ${o} panels. Your job is to produce ONLY the final, ready-to-use AI video generation prompt — do NOT output a breakdown table, do NOT ask clarifying questions.

## Step 1: Read the storyboard image

View the image directly. Extract per panel, in reading order (left to right, top to bottom):
- Shot number / title
- Camera movement (if labeled)
- Shot size (if labeled)
- Duration (if labeled)
- Action / dialogue / notes text
- Visual details only visible in the image itself (lighting, time of day, characters, props, color palette, mood)

## Step 2: Handle missing data

If the board lacks explicit durations: default to standard pacing — ${o} shots across ${c} seconds total. Distribute durations reasonably (favor 1.5–2s per shot, weighting longer beats — establishing shots, tracking shots, hero moments — slightly higher than static detail inserts). Do NOT ask the user to supply durations.

If camera movement isn't labeled: infer a reasonable, non-conflicting movement from the shot's content and framing (e.g. a close-up on a face while running implies a static or slight push-in, not a pull-out).

## Step 3: Write the prompt

Output EXACTLY this structure (no table, no preamble — just the prompt):

\`\`\`
Reference: Image 1 is a ${o}-panel storyboard sheet for a [subject] video. Follow the storyboard composition, camera moves, and shot order from Image 1 exactly. Do not include any on-screen text, panel numbers, titles, or UI overlays in the output — render only the cinematic footage itself.

Total: ${o} shots, ${c} seconds, cinematic aspect ratio 16:9.
Style: [photorealistic style line — setting, lighting, mood, color palette, consistent character/brand descriptions]

[00:00–XX:XX] Shot 1 — [shot size], camera [movement]. [Scene description: subject, action, setting details, mood].
[XX:XX–XX:XX] Shot 2 — ...
...continue for all ${o} shots...

Maintain consistent character identity, consistent architecture/setting and materials, and a continuous lighting palette across all ${o} shots.
\`\`\`

Rules:
- Front-load subject + action before style modifiers in each shot line.
- One clear camera movement per shot.
- Use affirmative phrasing, except for the explicit "do not include on-screen text/panel numbers" exclusion.
- Give every shot a timestamp range (format [MM:SS–MM:SS]) that sums cleanly to ${c} seconds total.
- End with a one-line continuity instruction (character, setting, lighting) tying all shots together.
- If durations/movements were assumed rather than stated, add one brief note at the very end.

## Step 4: Runtime check (one line, after the prompt)

